/*  slugDirectives -- THE DOTTED-PATH SWEEP, as a directives batch.

    WHAT IT DOES. The 2026-09-15 ruling on the comment convention is that the
    inline form is `// slug sentence?` -- slug required, sentence optional, and
    the DOTTED PATH IS NEVER INLINE. Two production files still carry the older
    spelling, which puts the path at the end of the line. This respells all of
    them to bare slugs, in place, and touches nothing else.

    ⚠ IT EDITS PRODUCTION SOURCE AND closeFile WRITES THE FILE BACK. The guide's
    copy-to-Tests workflow exists because directives have no undo. Here git is the
    undo: both files are committed and clean before this runs, so `git checkout --`
    restores either one. Do not run this against a dirty tree.

    THE FOUR CONSTRAINTS, and how each is met.
    1  SOURCE IS NAMED `source`. replaceAt and deleteAt hoist it by that literal
       name, so a differently-named field silently gives the hoist nothing and
       EVERY directive misses.
    2  getFile IS ONCE PER FIELD -- but a field can be RE-POINTED. closeFile,
       then assign the next path, then getFile again. Measured 2026-09-16; it is
       how one batch reaches two files. `source:=otherField` was tried first and
       exits 139.
    3  THE MARK ONLY ADVANCES, so the calls below are in FILE ORDER, and a
       directive whose text occurs N times is FIRED N TIMES -- each fire takes
       the next occurrence forward of the mark.
    4  fromThis NEVER CONTAINS `//` OR A QUOTE. It matches the comment INTERIOR
       only, because checkSkip swallows anything comment-shaped even inside
       quotes. The one entry whose sentence carries backticks is matched on the
       period before its path instead, so that sentence is never quoted at all.

    ⚠ EVERY fromThis IS END-ANCHORED WITH A NEWLINE, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES THE
    BATCH IDEMPOTENT RATHER THAN SINGLE-USE. Two of these paths ALSO appear in a
    BLOCK comment -- Commands.rtn:706 and ruleActions.rtn:1454 -- and block
    comments KEEP their dotted paths under the convention. Unanchored, the
    bare-path entry matched the block comment on a SECOND run and corrupted it:
    caught by the second-run check, not by reading. An inline key is always at
    end of line and a block-comment mention never is, so the newline is the
    discriminator. Do NOT drop it from a future entry.

    ⚠ THE REPLACEMENT TEXT CONTAINS ITS OWN fromThis PREFIX for the repeated
    entries, which is safe only because the mark has moved past the insert before
    the next search starts -- and because every fromThis ends with the PATH, which
    the replacement does not have. Neither fact is an accident; do not shorten a
    fromThis to just its sentence.

    Sentinel at the foot, single stop.  */
